function extractBooks(text, ignoreList = []) {
  const oldTestamentBooks = [
    'Genesis', 'Exodus', 'Leviticus', 'Numbers', 'Deuteronomy',
    'Joshua', 'Judges', 'Ruth', '1 Samuel', '2 Samuel', '1 Kings', '2 Kings',
    '1 Chronicles', '2 Chronicles', 'Ezra', 'Nehemiah', 'Esther', 'Job',
    'Psalms', 'Proverbs', 'Ecclesiastes', 'Song of Solomon', 'Isaiah', 'Jeremiah',
    'Lamentations', 'Ezekiel', 'Daniel', 'Hosea', 'Joel', 'Amos', 'Obadiah',
    'Jonah', 'Micah', 'Nahum', 'Habakkuk', 'Zephaniah', 'Haggai', 'Zechariah',
    'Malachi'
  ];

  const newTestamentBooks = [
    'Matthew', 'Mark', 'Luke', 'John', 'Acts', 'Romans', '1 Corinthians',
    '2 Corinthians', 'Galatians', 'Ephesians', 'Philippians', 'Colossians',
    '1 Thessalonians', '2 Thessalonians', '1 Timothy', '2 Timothy', 'Titus',
    'Philemon', 'Hebrews', 'James', '1 Peter', '2 Peter', '1 John', '2 John',
    '3 John', 'Jude', 'Revelation'
  ];

  const booksRegex = new RegExp(`\\b(${oldTestamentBooks.join('|')}|${newTestamentBooks.join('|')})\\b`, 'gi');

  const bookCounts = {};

  text.replace(booksRegex, (match) => {
    const cleanMatch = match.replace(/[^\w\s]/g, '').trim();
    if (!ignoreList.includes(cleanMatch)) {
      bookCounts[cleanMatch] = (bookCounts[cleanMatch] || 0) + 1;
    }
    return match;
  });

  if (Object.keys(bookCounts).length === 0) {
    console.log('No books found in text.');
  } else {
    console.log(bookCounts);
  }
}
```
This function takes a string of text and an optional ignore list as input. It defines arrays for the names of Old Testament and New Testament books, and creates a regular expression to match whole words that are in either array. The `replace` method is used to search for matches in the text, with a callback function that removes any punctuation or special characters from each match and increments its count in the `bookCounts` object if it's not in the ignore list.
At the end of the function, an error message is printed if no books were found in the text, otherwise the book counts are printed to the console. The regular expression uses the global (`g`) and case-insensitive (`i`) flags, and the `\\b` syntax ensures that only whole words are matched.

